---

## 12. Operating Sequence

A normal inspection cycle works as follows:

1. The rover starts and connects to the edge device.
2. The rover moves toward the inspection area.
3. An RFID tag identifies the current plot.
4. The camera is positioned toward the plant.
5. An image is captured.
6. The image is processed by the AI model on the smartphone.
7. The ESP32 provides the associated sensor readings.
8. The AI result and sensor information are combined into an inspection record.
9. The result is shown on the smartphone dashboard.
10. A short status summary is shown on the LCD.
11. The rover can continue to the next plot.

---

## 13. Architecture Goal

The main design goal is to keep the system modular.

The rover can operate as the physical sensing platform, while the smartphone provides the computing and user-interface layer. This means the AI model can be improved or replaced without having to redesign the motor and sensor control system.

The same approach also makes it possible to use the software on different capable edge devices in the future.

```text
              AGRICULTURAL INSPECTION SYSTEM

       ┌──────────────┐
       │ Cloud / PC    │
       │ Model Training│
       └───────┬──────┘
               │
          Trained Model
               │
               ▼
       ┌──────────────┐
       │ Edge Device  │
       │ AI + UI      │
       └───────┬──────┘
               │
          Communication
               │
               ▼
       ┌──────────────┐
       │ ESP32-S3     │
       │ Robot Control│
       └───────┬──────┘
               │
       ┌───────┼────────┐
       ▼       ▼        ▼
    Motors   Sensors    LCD
```

The architecture separates **model development, edge inference, robot control, and user interaction** so that each part can be developed and tested independently.
